WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:09.886
FAYE MOSS: Hello! Welcome back to Superstars in History , the talk show featuring exclusive interviews with people from

2
00:00:09.887 --> 00:00:16.742
the past. I'm your host, Faye Moss. Today's guest is one of history's greatest

3
00:00:16.743 --> 00:00:26.813
composers. He not only had a remarkable gift for creating complex, emotional music but also kept using that gift even while growing deaf. Please

4
00:00:26.814 --> 00:00:48.416
welcome a true musical genius—Ludwig van Beethoven! LUDWIG VAN

5
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:54.934
BEETHOVEN: Pardon me, but will this interview take very long? I'm quite a busy man, you know!

6
00:00:54.935 --> 00:01:00.058
FAYE MOSS: I'll . . . er . . . do my best to keep things moving

7
00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:03.937
along! Why don't we start with your famous sketchbooks. Did you—

8
00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:14.627
LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: No, no, no, no! Let's start with my childhood—that's when my musical gifts first appeared,

9
00:01:14.628 --> 00:01:18.818
of course! FAYE MOSS: No problem, Ludwig. Let's

10
00:01:18.819 --> 00:01:24.726
start wherever you like! LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: Thank you. I was

11
00:01:24.727 --> 00:01:31.211
born in Bonn, Germany, in 1770. Both my grandfather and father were singers at

12
00:01:31.212 --> 00:01:36.501
the court of the archbishop-elector. My father taught me how to play the violin

13
00:01:36.502 --> 00:01:41.775
and clavier, or keyboard. When I was about 10 or 11, I left school to

14
00:01:41.776 --> 00:01:51.389
focus on music. My father dreamed of turning me into a child prodigy like Wolfgang Mozart—he hoped to make money from my

15
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.445
talent. FAYE MOSS: I've heard you had a difficult relationship

16
00:01:55.446 --> 00:02:00.468
with your father. LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: Unfortunately, my father was a

17
00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:06.585
very strict and difficult man. As a child, I loved to make up my own music,

18
00:02:06.586 --> 00:02:12.729
but that infuriated him. "What are you scratching at now?" he'd yell when I improvised

19
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:19.585
on the violin. He also drank too much, and at night he'd yank me out of bed and force me to practice for

20
00:02:19.586 --> 00:02:29.372
hours. I never did become a child prodigy, but I learned by leaps and bounds when I became a student of Christian Gottlob Neefe. FAYE

21
00:02:29.373 --> 00:02:33.910
MOSS: Who was Christian Gottlob Neefe? I've never heard of

22
00:02:33.911 --> 00:02:41.923
him. LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: He was the court organist in Bonn—a very important position—and an

23
00:02:41.924 --> 00:02:51.230
excellent music teacher. At about age 12 or so, I became his assistant organist, and Neefe also encouraged my interest in

24
00:02:51.231 --> 00:02:55.457
composing. He even helped me to publish some of my original works!

25
00:02:55.458 --> 00:03:06.093
By the time I was 17, I'd made so much progress that the court gave me money to travel to Vienna, Europe's center of music. FAYE

26
00:03:06.094 --> 00:03:11.049
MOSS: That's where Mozart lived! Did you get to meet him while you were there?

27
00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:17.269
LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: That will remain my secret,

28
00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:26.162
young lady! I will tell you this—Mozart was heard to say that one day the world would be talking about

29
00:03:26.163 --> 00:03:31.355
me. My time in Vienna had to be cut short when my mother died.

30
00:03:31.356 --> 00:03:35.258
FAYE MOSS: I'm very sorry to hear

31
00:03:35.259 --> 00:03:39.093
that. LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: I stayed in Bonn for the next five

32
00:03:39.094 --> 00:03:47.531
years or so. By now my father couldn't hold a job because of his drinking, so I was my family's

33
00:03:47.532 --> 00:03:54.982
primary breadwinner. I continued to work at court, and wealthy parents hired me to teach piano to their

34
00:03:54.983 --> 00:04:00.564
children. That was a job I disliked intensely; I lacked the patience for it!

35
00:04:00.565 --> 00:04:07.595
However, as I met more influential people, more opportunities came my way. I

36
00:04:07.596 --> 00:04:14.818
performed at social events and was commissioned to write music for various occasions. FAYE MOSS:

37
00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:23.429
Your life changed forever when the famous composer Joseph Haydn visited Bonn around 1790, right? LUDWIG

38
00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:30.614
VAN BEETHOVEN: Yes, Haydn visited the city and happened to see some of my

39
00:04:30.615 --> 00:04:35.958
compositions. He offered to take me on as his student, and of course, I accepted.

40
00:04:35.959 --> 00:04:44.056
I left Bonn for Vienna in 1792 and never returned. FAYE

41
00:04:44.057 --> 00:04:49.576
MOSS: In Vienna did you focus only on composing music, or did you perform too?

42
00:04:49.577 --> 00:04:57.140
LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: I also performed on the piano, making my first public appearance there

43
00:04:57.141 --> 00:05:06.451
in 1795. During this time I wrote my first and second symphonies, as well as piano sonatas and music for string quartets.

44
00:05:06.452 --> 00:05:15.113
Most composers were writing music for the harpsichord, but I was different, composing music for the piano. I

45
00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:20.697
was enjoying great success when a personal tragedy struck. FAYE MOSS:

46
00:05:20.698 --> 00:05:23.594
You began losing your hearing?

47
00:05:23.595 --> 00:05:30.505
LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: Yes, to my horror, I was growing deaf, and it haunted me like

48
00:05:30.506 --> 00:05:40.181
a ghost! I was already a rather difficult person; my hearing problem made me more ill-tempered—some might say

49
00:05:40.182 --> 00:05:46.491
unbearable! FAYE MOSS: If you couldn't hear well, Ludwig, how in the world could you keep

50
00:05:46.492 --> 00:05:49.992
writing music? LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: I could still hear

51
00:05:49.993 --> 00:05:57.799
music in my head. I used the sketchbooks you mentioned to write down my ideas, which often came to me while walking in

52
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:09.470
the country. Because of my despair over my hearing loss, many of my compositions evoked powerful emotions, full of themes related to struggles and

53
00:06:09.471 --> 00:06:19.900
heroes. I wrote pieces such as the Piano Sonata no. 14, the Third Symphony, and my one and only opera— Fidelio

54
00:06:19.901 --> 00:06:24.909
. FAYE MOSS: Piano Sonata no. 14 is called the

55
00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:30.359
Moonlight Sonata sometimes. Some people say it was inspired by your love for a woman. Is

56
00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:39.379
that true? LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: Please, Faye, I'd rather not

57
00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:46.317
discuss something so personal! As for the Third Symphony, that was one of my

58
00:06:46.318 --> 00:06:57.170
most important works. It was longer than any previous symphony, and I dedicated it to Napoleon because I admired the democratic principles of the French

59
00:06:57.171 --> 00:07:07.671
Revolution. But later—after learning that Napoleon was acting like a king and calling himself emperor—I scrawled out my dedication!

60
00:07:07.672 --> 00:07:10.041
FAYE MOSS: When did you stop

61
00:07:10.042 --> 00:07:21.025
performing? LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: I believe my last public performance was in 1814; after that I turned all my

62
00:07:21.026 --> 00:07:27.190
energies to composing. FAYE MOSS: And during the next part of your life, you composed some of

63
00:07:27.191 --> 00:07:33.869
your most amazing works, transforming the symphony as a musical form. What can you tell

64
00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:42.190
us about this? LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: Yes, I was quite prolific in my later years, composing piano sonatas as

65
00:07:42.191 --> 00:07:53.641
well as my final symphony. The Ninth Symphony is very long, of course, and it incorporates choral singers, which was unusual and, dare I say, innovative

66
00:07:53.642 --> 00:07:59.417
in its time. When the symphony was first performed in Vienna, thunderous applause

67
00:07:59.418 --> 00:08:08.166
broke out. Because of my deafness, I didn't realize the audience was clapping until a performer turned me around to face them.

68
00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:12.070
I must say, the sight impressed even me!

69
00:08:12.071 --> 00:08:18.375
FAYE MOSS: Your compositions have endured for hundreds of years. Even today your music

70
00:08:18.376 --> 00:08:24.283
is— LUDWIG VAN BEETHOVEN: Faye, I've had quite enough. May I please return

71
00:08:24.284 --> 00:08:30.946
to history now? FAYE MOSS: Oh yes! Thank

72
00:08:30.947 --> 00:08:36.724
you, Ludwig, and good-bye! Ludwig van Beethoven died in

73
00:08:36.725 --> 00:08:41.506
1827 at age 57. Before Beethoven, vocal music was more

74
00:08:41.507 --> 00:08:49.017
popular than instrumental; he changed all of that with his compositions, especially his dazzling symphonies and

75
00:08:49.018 --> 00:08:56.187
piano music. Today his famous Fifth Symphony is among the most frequently played

76
00:08:56.188 --> 00:08:59.855
pieces. I hope you enjoyed meeting Ludwig van

77
00:08:59.856 --> 00:09:04.117
Beethoven! You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History

78
00:09:04.118 --> 00:09:16.879
. Don't miss my next interview with another fascinating person from the past!

